# {{ .computed.app_title }}

An app-chat project: the shared Substrate agentic app host, own identity and SQLite,
app-shell, kit-chat and kit-identity. Conversations and messages belong to the verified
principal. There is no anonymous chat and no provider connection in the starter.

## Start

```sh
go mod tidy
go run github.com/hollis-labs/substrate/cmd/substrate pins sync --app .
go mod tidy
cd frontend
npm install
npm run typecheck
npm run lint
npm run build
cd ..
go build -o {{ .inputs.repo_name }} ./cmd/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}
./{{ .inputs.repo_name }} identity setup --username owner
./{{ .inputs.repo_name }} serve --managed
```

Commit `go.sum` and `frontend/package-lock.json`. CI checks pins, generated wiring,
frontend types/lint/build, Go vet/race tests, both lint boundaries and public hygiene.
See `docs/identity-bootstrap.md` for scoped keys and `docs/runbook.md` for installed
operation, XDG paths, backup, release and rollback. Deploy settings are in `project.yaml`;
`folio deploy render . --plan` previews changes without deploying.

## Chat behavior

Create a conversation, send messages and reopen it to read saved history. The server
supplies roles and owner IDs; clients cannot select either. Conversation IDs belonging
to someone else return the same 404 as missing IDs. Plain text stays plain text.

By default the UI says **No assistant provider connected**. It does not invent a reply
or send data to an external service. To connect one, implement `service.Responder` and
call `service.New(...).WithResponder(provider)` in `cmd/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}/serve.go`
before serving. The hook receives only the authenticated owner's selected history;
keep provider credentials in operator configuration. It must honor context cancellation
and support concurrent calls. Concurrent sends can interleave replies; add a queue if
your application needs serialized turns. Failed assistant replies leave the user's
message saved and are reported separately, preventing automatic duplicate sends.

The API is GET/POST `/api/conversations` and GET/POST
`/api/conversations/{conversation}/messages`, requiring `{{ .inputs.repo_name }}.read`
or `{{ .inputs.repo_name }}.write`. The inherited `/api/items` example remains available
as a small service/transport example. The inherited MCP endpoint uses app-issued scopes;
chat is not exposed as MCP tools until you add authorized adapters to the service layer.

## Frontend development

For a separate Vite development origin, set `identity.allowed_origins` only in a local
`SUBSTRATE_CONFIG` override. Never add development origins to production configuration.
The published kit-identity renders sign-in and offline owner setup; the shared request
adapter owns cookies, CSRF headers and session expiry. All direct Hollis kits are explicit
BOM-version inputs; upgrade them together from the selected Substrate BOM.
